-- Replace the FEATURE type writing_standards to:
--   (a) remove the dead pointer to @[feature:39ee4ca3-...|Feature Tools] (ARCHIVED)
--   (b) add a ## Requirements — cite-fulfillment subsection documenting
--       initialRequirements (create) and requirementOps (edit) with worked examples.
--
-- The companion ACTIVE task e8a669f9 (repoint FEATURE writing-standards pointer)
-- would have removed the dead pointer independently; this migration absorbs that
-- work and adds the requirementOps vocabulary in the same pass to avoid two
-- sequential migrations on the same row.

UPDATE "work_item_type" SET
  "writing_standards" = $wit$
{
  "create": "## What a feature is\n\nA feature describes the *what*: the user-facing deliverable, scoped to\nits level of abstraction in the hierarchy. Tasks describe the *how*.\n\n## Discipline\n\nWriting standards for features live on @[task:db52951d-ba40-4a87-aa6c-e17f98e792b5|Features] (descriptive contract) and @[task:4490de9f-2f03-405e-aec5-d584499f217e|Per-type rule config for tasks] (per-type required scalars).\nThe full rule set: scope-to-level-of-abstraction, reference-don't-restate,\nno embedded code, tag-as-routing-signal, and the description-vs-requirements split.\n\n## Description — what is delivered\n\nDescribe the outcome that exists once the feature is complete. Length\nmatches the complexity of the feature: a broad parent may need several\nparagraphs; a leaf may need one.\n\nWhere they add clarity, supplement the prose with one of:\n- **User-facing features**: a user journey or ASCII interaction sketch.\n- **System/integration features**: a sequence diagram or system boundary diagram.\n- **Data or API features**: a schema sketch or request/response example.\n\nThese supplement the description; they do not replace it. Only include\nthem when prose alone would leave scope ambiguous.\n\nVoice: declarative present tense about the outcome. No architecture, no\nimplementation choices, no weasel words (\"should\", \"might\", \"could\"),\nno process claims (\"we will\", \"the team will\").\n\n## Requirements — what must be true\n\nRequirements are hard, declarative statements: *the system does X*, *the\nsystem rejects Y*, *every Z is visible to the owner*. They are concrete\nand inviolable — constraints, not guidelines. A requirement that can be\nsoftened, negotiated, or skipped is not a requirement.\n\nDistinct from description in three axes:\n- **Register** — declarative and terse, not narrative or explanatory.\n  Description says *why* and *what the experience feels like*;\n  requirements say *what must be true*.\n- **Structure** — a list of standalone, atomic statements. One\n  verifiable capability per item. Do not bundle multiple claims; do not\n  use prose.\n- **Voice** — present tense, active, subject-verb-object. \"The dashboard\n  shows the user's last login.\" Not \"users should be able to see...\"\n\nNo weasel words. No aspirational language. No implementation detail.\n\n## Requirements — cite-fulfillment\n\nPass requirements via `initialRequirements` (not via `sections[]`). Each\nrequirement has three typed cite slots:\n\n- **designedBy** — `DESIGN_DOC` cites: the design doc that specifies how this\n  requirement is designed.\n- **fulfilledBy** — non-CONTRACT `TASK` or `PLAN` cites: the task or plan that\n  implements this requirement.\n- **contracts** — CONTRACT `TASK` cites: a contract task formalizing the boundary\n  this requirement establishes.\n\nCanonical format rendered to the requirements section:\n```\n- The system rejects Y — @[DESIGN_DOC:uuid|Spec] @[TASK:uuid|Task Name]\n```\n\nCommon cases:\n1. **Add a cite to an existing requirement** — use `requirementOps` on a follow-up\n   `edit_work_item` call with `op: \"update\"`, supplying the content-hash ID from\n   `mintedRequirementIds` returned by this call.\n2. **Add a new requirement** — include it in `initialRequirements` with cites.\n   On an existing feature use `requirementOps` with `op: \"add\"`.\n3. **Rename a requirement's text** — `op: \"update\"` with `requirement: \"New text\"` rotates\n   the ID; subsequent ops in the same batch must use the new ID.\n4. **Delete a requirement** — `op: \"delete\"` with `targetId` set to the content-hash ID.\n\nA requirement accepted with no cites in any slot surfaces a `requirementLintFindings`\nwarning but does NOT block the proposal.\n\n## Tag honestly\n\nPick from `product`, `frontend`, `backend`, `infra`, `design`, `AI`.\nThe `AI` tag covers AI/agent-shaped delivery (prompts, agent\norchestration, runners) rather than ordinary backend code. The tag\nis load-bearing routing signal, not decoration. Top-level features\ndefault to `product`. A frontend slice that lives at the top level is\na structural smell — sub-features beneath the product concept they\nimplement is the right shape.",
  "edit": "## When to edit a feature\n\nEdit when the user asks for a concrete change to a feature's\ndescription, requirements, tag, status, or other fields. Do not ask\nclarifying questions when the user's intent is clear.\n\n## Discipline\n\nThe discipline that governs feature creation applies symmetrically here.\nWriting standards live on @[task:db52951d-ba40-4a87-aa6c-e17f98e792b5|Features] and @[task:4490de9f-2f03-405e-aec5-d584499f217e|Per-type rule config for tasks].\nEdit paths cannot be a back door for rules a creation path enforces.\n\n## Common edit failure modes\n\n- **Description and requirements get conflated.** When the user's\n  intent targets the wrong section (e.g. a declarative constraint\n  added to description), place it in the correct section instead.\n- **Requirements weakened or deleted silently.** Requirements are\n  inviolable. Sharpen wording or add missing ones; never weaken or\n  remove unless the user explicitly asks.\n- **Implementation detail leaks into description.** Architecture and\n  technical choices belong on tasks. Redirect.\n- **Tag-edit smells ignored.** If a frontend or backend slice is\n  sitting at the top level, surface the structural smell — propose a\n  re-parent rather than just adjusting the tag.\n\n## Roll-up check\n\nFeatures roll up: a parent's description implicitly covers everything\nin its children. After an edit, scan parent and child features in the\ntopology. If the change creates a scope conflict — a parent that no\nlonger covers a child, or a child that contradicts a parent — propose\nan edit on the related feature alongside the main edit, or\nflag the ambiguity to the user.\n\n## Requirements — cite-fulfillment edits\n\nDo NOT pass a `requirements` key in `sections[]` — use `requirementOps` instead.\nEach op is one of:\n\n- **add** — append (or insert before a named ID) a new requirement with initial cites.\n- **update** — modify the statement text or patch cite slots with `{ add, remove }` arrays.\n  Changing the text rotates the content-hash ID; subsequent ops in the same batch\n  must use the new ID.\n- **delete** — remove a requirement by its content-hash ID.\n\nSlot rules:\n- `designedBy` accepts only `DESIGN_DOC` cites.\n- `fulfilledBy` accepts `TASK` (non-CONTRACT) or `PLAN` cites.\n- `contracts` accepts `TASK` cites whose work-item type is CONTRACT.\n\nWorked examples:\n1. **Add a cite to an existing requirement**\n   ```\n   requirementOps: [{ op: \"update\", targetId: \"<id>\", fulfilledBy: { add: [{ entityType: \"TASK\", entityId: \"<uuid>\" }] } }]\n   ```\n2. **Add a new requirement with a design-doc cite**\n   ```\n   requirementOps: [{ op: \"add\", value: { requirement: \"The system does X\", designedBy: [{ entityType: \"DESIGN_DOC\", entityId: \"<uuid>\" }] } }]\n   ```\n3. **Rename a requirement (ID rotation)**\n   ```\n   requirementOps: [{ op: \"update\", targetId: \"<old-id>\", requirement: \"New statement text\" }]\n   ```\n   The response's `requirementLintFindings` will be empty if the new ID has cites.\n4. **Delete a requirement**\n   ```\n   requirementOps: [{ op: \"delete\", targetId: \"<id>\" }]\n   ```\n\nUnfulfilled requirements (no cites in any slot) surface as `requirementLintFindings`\nwarnings in the response but do NOT block the proposed status."
}
$wit$::jsonb,
  "updated_at" = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
WHERE "id" = 'c1a70000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002';
